  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1200 AM CST Thu Nov 5 2026\n\nToday: Rain Finally Comes to an End\n\nThe stacked low responsible for our prolonged stretch of wet \nand dreary weather to start November finally opens into a wave \nand lifts northeastward early this morning. The lower \ntropospheric deformation zone/surface low gradually elongates \nand dissipates over the region by sunset with the departure of \nits upper level support. The accompanying rain showers likewise \nwane and shift east through the day. Overall additional rainfall\namounts for most locales look to be less than a tenth of an \ninch. Low clouds will be a bit slower to dissipate with a \ntrailing upper tropospheric wave sliding down across the MN \nArrowhead and into Wisconsin later today and tonight, keeping \ncyclonic flow in place into Friday afternoon.\n\nFriday into Next Week: Drier, Seasonal Temps till Sat, Warmer \nEarly Next Week\n\nLarge scale descent in the wake of our the upper wave will lead\nto clearing skies and cooler temperatures for Friday. The \nentire region will almost certainly (>95%) drop below freezing \nFriday night into Saturday morning. Users should note that, \nsince the growing season has already ended, no Freeze Watches, \nFrost Advisories, or Freeze Warnings will be issued until \nspring.\n\nAfter a northern and southern stream wave pass well to our \nnortheast and southwest, respectively, on Sunday, heights aloft \nrise into the new work week. Temperatures will once again rise \nto above normal for this time of year, likely (70-90% per 05.01z\nNBM) above 60 for Monday and the upper 50s into the middle of \nnext week. The next chance of noteworthy precipitation may not \narrive until the end of next week at the earliest.",
